# Who is asked to review what.
#
# GitHub reads this file to decide whose review a pull request requests. It only
# has an effect if the branch requires review from code owners, so today it is
# mostly a statement of intent: one maintainer, and everything is theirs.
#
# It is written out rather than left as a single `*` line because the interesting
# part is what happens when a second pair of hands arrives. The patterns below
# name the areas where a change is easy to make and hard to check, so that the
# person who understands that area is asked about it. Add specific patterns above
# the catch-all as they become true; until then they all resolve to the
# maintainer.

# Every file, unless something more specific below matches.
*                       @James-HoneyBadger

# The engine that runs the languages, and the reference the ports are checked
# against. A mistake here is a wrong answer in a language rather than a broken
# window, which is much harder to notice.
/native/bridge/         @James-HoneyBadger

# The curriculum. It is data, so nothing exercises it by accident -- a lesson
# nobody can finish would sit in the tree looking correct.
/native/bridge/time_warp/features/lessons.json  @James-HoneyBadger

# The lessons and the tools that drive them: what a learner sees, and the marking
# a teacher trusts.
/docs/lessons/          @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tools/learn.py  @James-HoneyBadger
/native/tools/teach.py  @James-HoneyBadger

# The gates themselves. A change to these decides what "green" means for every
# other change, so it deserves a second look even when it looks harmless.
/native/tests/          @James-HoneyBadger
/.github/workflows/     @James-HoneyBadger

# The documents that are read as statements about the product. Each of these has
# claimed something untrue at least once.
/README.md              @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md   @James-HoneyBadger
/docs/ROADMAP.md        @James-HoneyBadger
/CHANGELOG.md           @James-HoneyBadger
